{
  "//": [
    "Web app manifest. It exists for one reason: to declare a scope covering both",
    "screens, so iOS keeps the whole app in its standalone window instead of throwing",
    "the crew into an overlay browser the moment they tap between HUD and Race.",

    "Modern iOS decides standalone-versus-browser by the manifest's scope. With no",
    "manifest it infers one from the single URL that was saved to the Home Screen, so",
    "whichever page was saved stayed full screen and the other one always opened with",
    "browser chrome and a Done button, and coming back went full screen again. That is",
    "reported behaviour from the boat, on the phone. The iPad, on iOS 12, predates scope",
    "enforcement and was unaffected, which is why the two devices disagreed.",

    "Every URL here is relative, and that is load-bearing. scope and start_url resolve",
    "against the manifest's own URL, and app.py serves this from the app root, so behind",
    "nginx './' becomes /race/ and on the app's own port it becomes /. One file, correct",
    "in both, with no host and no prefix written down anywhere (CLAUDE.md).",

    "It must be served from the app root and not from static/, or './' would resolve to",
    "/race/static/ and exclude the very pages it is meant to cover.",

    "apple-mobile-web-app-capable stays in both pages alongside this. It is what iOS 12",
    "on the iPad reads, and it ignores this file entirely.",

    "No icons declared. iOS takes the Home Screen icon from apple-touch-icon, or a",
    "screenshot when there is none, which is the situation today. Worth adding, but an",
    "icon is a separate job from getting the navigation to stay inside the app."
  ],

  "name": "Enchantee racing",
  "short_name": "Race",
  "scope": "./",
  "start_url": "./",
  "display": "standalone",
  "background_color": "#000000",
  "theme_color": "#000000",

  "icons": [
    { "src": "static/icon-512.png", "sizes": "512x512", "type": "image/png" },
    { "src": "static/icon-180.png", "sizes": "180x180", "type": "image/png" }
  ]
}
